{"repositoryId":395723117,"availability":"available","full":"plynoi/docker-multi-stage-size","name":"docker-multi-stage-size","description":"This repository shows size comparision between Docker single-stage and multi-stage builds.","category":"Developer tools","language":"Python","stars":2,"forks":0,"created":"2021-08-13T16:36:12Z","updated":"2026-10-18T13:42:20Z","license":"MIT","branch":"main","homepage":null,"topics":[],"demo":"https://watasalim.vercel.app/","color":"#323057","ink":"#d6caff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-18T18:17:49.209Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-18T18:17:49.209Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-18T18:17:49.209Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-18T18:17:49.209Z","stars":2,"forks":0}],"technologies":["Python"],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Docker is an open platform for developing, shipping, and running applications in a loosely isolated and consistent replication environment.","When creating Docker applications, it is a good practice to keep your images lightweight. There are many techniques to reduce the image size such as using .dockerignore file, optimize layers, using a small based image (such as -alpine) , etc. The multi-stage builds is the one if the useful way to reduce image size and make readable Dockerfile.","The multi-stage builds let you organize Dockerfile by split your Dockerfile into multiple stages with multiple FROM instructions. Each FROM instruction can use a different base, and each of them begins a new stage of the build. You can selectively copy artifacts/outputs from the previous stage to another, leaving behind everything you don’t want in the final image. The final image will contain only things you really need to run."],"features":["Last update: Aug 2021","Environment: Windows WSL","Prerequisite: Docker and Internet connection"],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/plynoi/docker-multi-stage-size/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"98c4364714d1837ab021dae0151ed181fdfa13085259cd70ec8bd1fe7fbd1dd7","extractedAt":"2026-